     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\">Following up on this: my research has led me to the SP package, which according to the leaflet documentation, plays well with leaflet().  I am struggling with converting my df below to a kosher sp::spatialPolygons object, especially given that my use case has n-shapes, and not just two in the MRE presented.<div><br></div><div>Again, any guidance for a sp-based solution sending my df to a leaflet friendly object is appreciated.</div><div><br></div><div>an alternative would be to loop over (lapply(), maybe?) addPolygons in a pipline, but I&#39;ve been unsuccessful there as well. <br><br>On Wednesday, January 20, 2016 at 4:52:16 PM UTC-6, ArjunaCap wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Given the following df:<div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span>df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>lat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span> lng </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.17</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span> shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;triangle&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;triangle&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;triangle&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;square&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;square&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;square&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;square&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span> df<br>   lat    lng    shape<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.17</span><span> triangle<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.0</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.20</span><span> triangle<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.0</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span> triangle<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.20</span><span>   square<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span>   square<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.0</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.14</span><span>   square<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">36.0</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">94.20</span><span>   square</span></div></code></div><div><div><br></div></div><div><br></div><div>suppose I want to overlay the df shapes onto a leaflet map via addPolygons().  Either of the following work well:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span>df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> filter</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;triangle&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> leaflet</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> addPolygons</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>lat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> lng </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lng</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br><br><br><br><br>df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> filter</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;square&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> leaflet</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> addPolygons</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>lat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> lng </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lng</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>If, however, the order of that lat/lng arguments aren&#39;t just so (ensured by subsetting via filter() above), you get erratic plotting, eg:</div><div><br><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span>df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> leaflet</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span> addPolygons</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>lat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> lng </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lng</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div>How can I solve this?</div><div><br></div><div>The bigger picture is that I have a much larger df for which I want to have a user select regions in a shiny app, but I need each region plotted discreetly, regardless of how many or which are selected.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks in advance.</div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
